<p class="lt-chem-36404">Addition of a hydride nucleophile to the carbonyl carbon of the amide produces a tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate. A Lewis acid-base interaction occurs between the alkoxide (Lewis Base) and AlH<sub>3</sub> (Lewis acid) forming a complex with an O-Al bond. The nitrogen lone pair forms a double bond with the previous carbonyl carbon ejecting a metal oxide species (e.g. [H3Al&ndash;O]<sup>2&ndash;</sup>) as a leaving group, and while forming an iminium double bond. Addition of a second equivalent of hydride nucleophile to the iminium carbon produces an amine.</p>

